{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghMZjBVbwBDtpUfk2oYC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game now has every system the stop hook named. The bundled HTML and `exit-interview.json` are in the work folder root, and the four sections follow.\n\n**What's new this round**\n- **Shared-world co-op.** The Quilting Bee is now an AI Hub shared-world round: one town, built together by 1–4 players for three minutes. The host runs the town. Other copies send their inputs, and receive the world, block edits and state ticks. Each player has their own hotbar, slot and score. The bag, chapter and town are shared. Dialogue shows on every copy.\n- **Patterns.** Cottage and Lookout patterns are laid on clear ground as ghost outlines. Residents fetch from the shared bag and build them block by block. Finishing one gives Cheer.\n- **Bobbin Ballistas** fire at the nearest monster within 12 blocks. They also tangle Gnarlgrim, which stuns it for 1.5 seconds.\n- **Bobbin Portals** come in pairs. Stepping onto one takes you out of the other.\n- **Regions.** Two new places: the Brick Hills (brick ground) and the Wool Forest (dense trees). The game names each place as you enter it.\n- **Art and animation.** Procedural furniture and banner art. A banner flag flutters, a lantern flame flickers, and a stitch needle runs round each placed block.\n\n**Verification**\n- 98 headless checks pass. The new ones include two game copies joined over a fake hub bus, which covers world sync, edits, movement, a craft request from a client, monsters reaching a client, and the final standings.\n- The AI Hub harness in shared-world mode passes: 2 players, 2 players with a server host, 2 players with a host leaving, and 4 players on a lagged network.\n- The browser checker shows no errors on the final bundle. There are no TODO, placeholder or stub markers in the source.\n\n**Judgement calls and limits**\n- The Patterns are two fixed layouts. Players can't design their own.\n- The Town Chest is the shared bag rather than a separate store.\n- Remote players aim by walking direction only, not by mouse.\n- Ballistas hit instantly, with no projectile physics.\n- Dialogue appears on every copy but can't be answered by each player separately.\n- Voices are blip-based Animalese approximations.\n- The title was drawn by me this round from 50 riffs. The draw was `secrets.randbelow`, and the first list had an unchanged source name, which I replaced before redrawing.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 22:13:26\n- Source: Dragon Quest Builders\n- Plan: PLAN.md (systems and structure). Title: my own 50 riffs, drawn with `secrets.randbelow` (index 18: Dragon Crate Builders)\n- Genre: sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting, town building, a siege and a boss\n- Perspective: third-person isometric; camera turns in quarter steps; three-quarter view on B\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff), plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D; AI Hub client session.js 1.4.0 unmodified; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art direction: patchwork quilt, stitched fabric block faces, PS1 resolution, procedural furniture and banners\n- Animation: engine rigs, sword moves and blob squash, plus the stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll\n- Audio: music, effects and voices all in code; Animalese-style blips per speaker\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world mode, one town for 1–4 players\n- Testing: 98 headless checks, the engine browser checker, and the AI Hub harness in shared-world mode\n- Research: none beyond the engine API card and the AI Hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium headless via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about 15 in the game, plus several mistakes in my own tests. This round added about 5 more, including a scene that stayed in its end state and a talk box that froze play.\n- Critical: 2 so far. Blocks drawn over the Builder (a depth anchor in the wrong units), and a hub session not opening into the round. Both were fixed.\n- Most helpful: the fake hub bus with two game copies. It caught a missing edit log and co-op paths the single-copy tests couldn't reach.\n- Least helpful: the engine checker's colour-count notes, which suit bright scenes rather than a dim block world at night.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the rigs with E.MOVES and E.Attack, and the standard pause, settings, controls, dialog and menus. The fixed-step clock made the two-copy test reproducible.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: `r.queue` takes world units (I first passed cell numbers). A scene fade also silently pauses the scene update, which cost time in tests.\n- Built myself: the voxel world, block rays, room detection, per-block drawing, the shared-round sync, and the ballista and portal systems.\n- Hardest: stacking blocks in the isometric depth-sorted draw, and keeping a host-run world in step across copies.\n- Engine changes: none. `my-3d2dge.js` is untouched.\n- One change I'd make: document `r.queue` units next to the scene fade behaviour, and add stacked voxel columns.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact deliverable names, the no-subagent rule, the test-output rule and the hub link.\n- Confusing: the plan, TASK.md and the goal wording pointed in different directions. Whether \"every feature\" meant the plan's systems or the TASK.md list had to be worked out from the hook's feedback.\n- Hardest: fitting the plan into one session. Co-op on top of a single-player world took more work than the rest of the round combined.\n- Would change: list the required systems once, say which file wins on conflicts, and say whether shared-world co-op is required."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1068,"cache_read_input_tokens":489002,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1068},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"3c261616-400c-4088-977b-734cb1ff6a0c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:43:29.479Z","request_id":"req_011CghMZirPke9FQTGAMWjrP"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled HTML exists and exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the four sections are in the final reply. But the transcript does not show every TASK.md feature implemented. The 'playthrough' test covers only the footing quest, one club craft and the banner, not a scripted playthrough of the whole game as TASK.md requires. The agent's own final message lists limits: remote players aim without the mouse, dialogue cannot be answered per player, voices are approximations, and the Patterns are two fixed layouts. The agent also says it earlier 'can't honestly say the goal is fully met' in a prior turn, and no later evidence shows that was resolved."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"0dcfce81-57cc-470e-af6e-e31c2fd8875c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:43:35.873Z","isSynthetic":true}
