{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghMxEft2bK6rURq1ckww","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The last open items are closed in code and tested, and the bundle is rebuilt. I'm still not claiming more than the evidence shows, so the feature map below says what each TASK.md item rests on.\n\n**Closed this round**\n- **Animalese.** The engine's dialog now calls an `onLetter` hook, which I added and marked `// DQ:`. Each letter of a speech is sung as its own pitched syllable: vowels are rounder tones, consonants short clicks, punctuation a low pause, spaces a breath. Words aren't produced. The engine change is the only one, and it's logged in the About pages and the exit interview.\n- **Remote mouse aim.** A remote player now aims with the pointer the client sent, not the host's pointer. Tested over the two-copy bus.\n- **Patterns.** Four fixed layouts now: cottage, lookout, shed and lodge. Each is data, so adding one is a data change. Residents build them from the shared bag.\n\n**Still not built, by design**\n- Patterns are fixed layouts. Players can't author their own.\n- Dialogue has no choices in this game, so \"answering per player\" doesn't arise. Every copy reads the same lines. I don't think TASK.md asks for choices.\n- The voices are pitched syllables, not speech-like phonemes. That's the closest I got to Animalese without a speech synthesiser.\n\n**Evidence, by TASK.md item**\n- **Single HTML bundle:** built into this folder's root, and it runs in Chromium with no errors.\n- **Genre, perspective, tech, art:** the isometric voxel world, the engine's rigs, and procedural furniture and banners. The browser check shows the scene rendering.\n- **Animation:** the engine's rigs and sword moves, plus a stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll. Exercised by the chapter playthrough.\n- **Audio:** five songs and sound effects in code, and per-letter Animalese. The Animalese check counts at least 20 voiced letters, at least six distinct pitches, and both vowel and consonant shapes.\n- **Dev mode:** Dev Tools from the pause menu, with live sliders, cheats and a reset. The suite checks opening, changing a slider, reset and cancel.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 seconds idle, and any press exits it.\n- **Controls:** keyboard, mouse and pad mapping; PlayStation or Xbox glyphs by setting; mouse aim and click. The Xbox setting has a check, but I haven't verified the glyph text on screen.\n- **Touch-only notice:** shown on touch-only devices, with Options as the settings button. Checked in an emulated touch screenshot.\n- **Co-op Quilting Bee:** the AI Hub shared-world round. The two-copy test covers world sync, edits, movement, craft requests, monsters, remote aim and standings. The AI Hub harness passes in shared-world mode with 1, 2 and 4 players, a server host, and a host leaving.\n- **Whole game:** a scripted playthrough from the first quest to the end screen, with god mode off.\n- **Save and load:** through the engine's slots, with a round-trip test.\n- **About & Credits:** the source information, feedback and controls pages.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses and fills every field.\n\nThe suite runs 115 checks and all pass. No TODO, placeholder or stub markers are in the source.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 22:13:26\n- Source: Dragon Quest Builders\n- Plan: PLAN.md (systems and structure). Title: my own 50 riffs, drawn with `secrets.randbelow` (index 18: Dragon Crate Builders)\n- Genre: sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting, town building, a siege and a boss\n- Perspective: third-person isometric; camera turns in quarter steps; three-quarter view on B\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff) with one logged hook; plain JavaScript; Canvas 2D; AI Hub client session.js 1.4.0 unmodified; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art direction: patchwork quilt, stitched fabric block faces, PS1 resolution, procedural furniture and banners\n- Animation: engine rigs, sword moves and blob squash, plus a stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll\n- Audio: music, effects and per-letter Animalese, all in code\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world mode, one town for 1–4 players\n- Testing: 115 headless checks (including a whole-chapter playthrough and a fake hub bus joining two copies), the engine browser checker, and the AI Hub harness in shared-world mode\n- Research: none beyond the engine API card and the AI Hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium headless via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about 18 in the game, plus several mistakes in my own tests. This round found the Animalese gap (blips weren't Animalese) and the remote-aim gap.\n- Critical: 2 so far, both fixed. Blocks drawn over the Builder (a depth anchor in the wrong units), and a hub session not opening into the round.\n- Most helpful: the whole-chapter playthrough and the two-copy test on a fake bus.\n- Least helpful: the engine checker's colour-count notes, which suit bright scenes rather than a dim block world at night.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the rigs with E.MOVES and E.Attack, and the standard pause, settings, controls, dialog and menus.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: `r.queue` takes world units, and I first passed cell numbers. A scene fade silently holds the scene update, and dialog plays one fixed sound per letter, which is why Animalese needed a hook.\n- Built myself: the voxel world, block rays, room detection, per-block drawing, the shared-round sync, ballistas, portals, patterns, and the Animalese voicing.\n- Hardest: stacking blocks in the isometric depth-sorted draw, and keeping a host-run world in step across copies.\n- Engine changes: one. Dialog gained an `onLetter(index, talk)` hook, marked `// DQ:`.\n- One change I'd make: a per-letter voice option in the dialog, so games don't need the hook.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact deliverable names, the no-subagent rule, the test-output rule and the hub link.\n- Confusing: the plan, TASK.md and the goal wording pointed different ways. Whether \"every feature\" meant the plan's systems or the TASK.md list had to be worked out from the hook's feedback, and the hook kept asking for evidence rather than statements.\n- Hardest: fitting the plan into one session. Co-op on a single-player world took more work than the rest of the round combined.\n- Would change: list the required features once, with the evidence expected for each, and say whether shared-world co-op and player-authored patterns are required."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1030,"cache_read_input_tokens":534908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1030},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"45f1651d-28b9-45e0-8f50-ecc87a62177f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:48:36.230Z","request_id":"req_011CghMxEK3nVXu1AMaZdua5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied on transcript evidence. The bundle and exit-interview.json exist and the four sections are present, but the final message itself concedes gaps against TASK.md: the voices are 'pitched syllables, not speech-like phonemes' and the closest to Animalese the agent got, with 'Patterns are fixed layouts' and 'Players can't author their own'. TASK.md's Animalese requirement and the 'every feature implemented' criterion are not clearly met, and the Xbox glyph text was never verified on screen. The agent also asserts 'I believe every TASK.md feature is now implemented' without a per-feature check of the Dev Mode sliders, Attract demo, or Bee playability in the final bundle, so completion rests on assertion rather than evidence."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"3c27ecdb-6b69-4016-9eb8-8bbe1d38683d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:48:37.927Z","isSynthetic":true}
