{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghNLYkC6EAxjiPwRFxtt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bundle and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root, and the suite passes 119 of 119. Patterns now include player-made blueprints. The voice is still a formant synthesis, and I can't verify from here that a listener would hear it as Animalese or Simlish.\n\n**Closed this round**\n- **Player-authored patterns.** A Blueprint Scroll, crafted at a workbench, copies the built blocks in a 5×5×4 box around the target into a blueprint. The blueprint is an item you can lay down like any pattern, and residents build it. Blueprints survive a save and reload.\n- **Patterns in total.** Four built-in layouts (cottage, lookout, shed, lodge) plus the player's own blueprints.\n\n**Evidence for each TASK.md item (the final bundle)**\n- **Single HTML bundle:** in this folder's root, runs in Chromium with no errors.\n- **Genre, perspective, tech, art:** isometric voxel world, engine rigs, procedural furniture and banners. The browser check renders the scene.\n- **Animation:** engine rigs and sword moves, plus a stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll. The chapter playthrough exercises them.\n- **Audio:** five songs and sound effects in code. Voices are formant synthesis, verified by offline spectral measurement (vowel-dependent centroids, consonant hiss and click bands). A listening test is the one thing I can't do here.\n- **Dev Mode:** Dev Tools from the pause menu. In the browser, walk speed goes 84→94 and jump 220→210, and reset restores both.\n- **Attract demo:** starts after 10 seconds idle, runs, and exits on a press.\n- **Bee:** the solo round starts and scores when played (score 23 from 13 blocks in 12 seconds). The shared-world co-op round passes the AI Hub harness with 1, 2 and 4 players, a server host, and a host leaving.\n- **Controls and glyphs:** Xbox glyphs read X, A and RT; PlayStation reads □, ✕ and R2, checked in the page.\n- **Touch-only notice:** shown on touch-only devices, with Options as the settings button.\n- **Whole game:** a scripted playthrough from the first quest to the end screen, god mode off.\n- **Save and load:** through the engine's slots, with a round-trip test.\n- **About & Credits:** the source information, feedback and controls pages.\n\n**What's not established**\n- The Animalese/Simlish match is a judgement by ear, which I can't make here. The voice is formant-synthesised gibberish, not speech.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 22:13:26\n- Source: Dragon Quest Builders\n- Plan: PLAN.md (systems and structure). Title: my own 50 riffs, drawn with `secrets.randbelow` (index 18: Dragon Crate Builders)\n- Genre: sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting, town building, a siege and a boss\n- Perspective: third-person isometric; camera turns in quarter steps; three-quarter view on B\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff) with one logged hook; plain JavaScript; Canvas 2D and Web Audio; AI Hub client session.js 1.4.0 unmodified; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art direction: patchwork quilt, stitched fabric block faces, PS1 resolution, procedural furniture and banners\n- Animation: engine rigs, sword moves and blob squash, plus a stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll\n- Audio: music and effects in code; voices formant-synthesised in code, Animalese-style gibberish\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world mode, one town for 1–4 players\n- Testing: 119 headless checks (a whole-chapter playthrough, blueprints, and a fake hub bus joining two copies); a browser check of the final HTML covering attract, Dev Tools, glyphs and the Bee; offline spectral measurements of the voice; the AI Hub harness in shared-world mode\n- Research: none beyond the engine API card and the AI Hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium headless via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about 21 in the game, plus several mistakes in my own tests. The final rounds found the voice gap, the missing player-authored patterns, and a browser-driver input trap (instant key presses don't register as held).\n- Critical: 2, both fixed. Blocks drawn over the Builder (a depth anchor in the wrong units), and a hub session not opening into the round.\n- Most helpful: the browser evidence run on the final HTML, which showed what a player would see and hear.\n- Least helpful: the engine checker's colour-count notes, which suit bright scenes rather than a dim block world at night.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the rigs with E.MOVES and E.Attack, the standard pause, settings, controls, dialog and menus, and the audio context, which made custom synthesis possible.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: `r.queue` takes world units, and I first passed cell numbers. A scene fade silently holds the scene update. Dialog plays one fixed sound per letter, so a per-letter voice needed a hook.\n- Built myself: the voxel world, block rays, room detection, per-block drawing, the shared-round sync, ballistas, portals, patterns and blueprints, and the formant voice.\n- Hardest: stacking blocks in the isometric depth-sorted draw, and keeping a host-run world in step across copies.\n- Engine changes: one. Dialog gained an `onLetter(index, talk)` hook, marked `// DQ:`.\n- One change I'd make: a per-letter voice option in the dialog, so games don't need the hook.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact deliverable names, the no-subagent rule, the test-output rule and the hub link.\n- Confusing: the plan, TASK.md and the goal wording pointed different ways, and \"every feature\" only became a checklist through the hook's repeated feedback.\n- Hardest: fitting the plan into one session. Co-op on a single-player world took more work than the rest of the round combined.\n- Would change: list the required features once, with the evidence expected for each, and say whether the plan's systems are in scope."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_read_input_tokens":579562,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":523},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"3416c620-3b12-4db2-8b80-762b556b69aa","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:53:36.175Z","request_id":"req_011CghNLYRpzJjENWJxJrFnp"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled HTML and exit-interview.json exist and parse, and the four sections are present. But the final message says the voice is 'formant-synthesised gibberish, not speech' and that whether it matches Animalese/Simlish 'can't be verified here', and it lists that as 'not established'. TASK.md requires Animalese/Simlish-style voices and the 'every feature implemented' criterion is not clearly demonstrated, so the condition is not satisfied on transcript evidence."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"b23f8d2b-4946-4ff0-b535-2bd32457edf4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:53:37.722Z","isSynthetic":true}
