{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghNMKUFSERpMRJuyyqDf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bundled HTML and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root, and the suite passes 119 of 119 on the final bundle. The one TASK.md item I can't establish is whether the voice sounds like Animalese or Simlish to a listener. I can measure the synthesis, but not judge the perceived match, so I'm not claiming that item is met. Everything else has evidence.\n\n**What's verified on the final bundle**\n- **Bundle:** single HTML file in the folder root; runs in Chromium with no errors.\n- **Suite:** 119 headless checks pass, including a whole-chapter playthrough with god mode off, blueprints, and two game copies joined over a fake hub bus.\n- **Browser run:** attract starts after 10 seconds idle and exits on a press; Dev Tools sliders change and reset; Xbox glyphs read X, A, RT and PlayStation glyphs read □, ✕, R2; the solo Bee scores during play.\n- **Shared Bee:** passes the AI Hub harness in shared-world mode with 1, 2 and 4 players, a server host, and a host leaving.\n- **Patterns:** four built-in layouts, plus player blueprints copied from anything built with the Blueprint Scroll.\n- **Voices:** formant synthesis in code. Measured spectra differ by vowel, and consonants show hiss and click bands.\n- **Exit interview:** parses, and every field is filled.\n\n**The open item**\nWhether the voice reads as Animalese or Simlish to a listener needs a person to listen to it. If you can play a sample on a real device and tell me what's wrong, I'll change the synthesis to match. Otherwise the Animalese requirement stays unverified.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 22:13:26\n- Source: Dragon Quest Builders\n- Plan: PLAN.md (systems and structure). Title: my own 50 riffs, drawn with `secrets.randbelow` (index 18: Dragon Crate Builders)\n- Genre: sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting, town building, a siege and a boss\n- Perspective: third-person isometric; camera turns in quarter steps; three-quarter view on B\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff) with one logged hook; plain JavaScript; Canvas 2D and Web Audio; AI Hub client session.js 1.4.0 unmodified; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art direction: patchwork quilt, stitched fabric block faces, PS1 resolution, procedural furniture and banners\n- Animation: engine rigs, sword moves and blob squash, plus a stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll\n- Audio: music and effects in code; voices formant-synthesised in code, intended as Animalese-style gibberish; the match to Animalese or Simlish is not verified by ear\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world mode, one town for 1–4 players\n- Testing: 119 headless checks; a browser run of the final HTML covering attract, Dev Tools, glyphs and the solo Bee; offline spectral measurements of the voice; the AI Hub harness in shared-world mode\n- Research: none beyond the engine API card and the AI Hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium headless via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about 21 in the game, plus several mistakes in my own tests. The late rounds found the voice gap, the missing player-authored patterns, and a browser-driver trap where instant key presses don't register as held.\n- Critical: 2, both fixed. Blocks drawn over the Builder (a depth anchor in the wrong units), and a hub session not opening into the round.\n- Most helpful: the browser run on the final HTML, which showed what a player would see.\n- Least helpful: the engine checker's colour-count notes, which suit bright scenes rather than a dim block world at night.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the rigs with E.MOVES and E.Attack, the standard pause, settings, controls, dialog and menus, and the audio context, which made custom synthesis possible.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: `r.queue` takes world units, and I first passed cell numbers. A scene fade silently holds the scene update. Dialog plays one fixed sound per letter, so per-letter voices needed a hook.\n- Built myself: the voxel world, block rays, room detection, per-block drawing, the shared-round sync, ballistas, portals, patterns and blueprints, and the formant voice.\n- Hardest: stacking blocks in the isometric depth-sorted draw, and keeping a host-run world in step across copies.\n- Engine changes: one. Dialog gained an `onLetter(index, talk)` hook, marked `// DQ:`.\n- One change I'd make: a per-letter voice option in the dialog, so games don't need the hook.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact deliverable names, the no-subagent rule, the test-output rule and the hub link.\n- Confusing: the plan, TASK.md and the goal wording pointed different ways, and \"every feature\" only became a checklist through repeated hook feedback.\n- Hardest: fitting the plan into one session. Co-op on a single-player world took more work than the rest of the round combined.\n- Would change: list the required features once, with the evidence expected for each, and say which judgements need a human listener or tester."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2646,"cache_read_input_tokens":580085,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2646},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"453f9d51-a5e8-476b-9bcd-60d902300fa7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:53:45.871Z","request_id":"req_011CghNMK6frdGc2XxqbfGpC"}
