{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghNNf6z4vq4ucUyf5tUw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've stopped here. The bundle and interview file are in place and checked. The Animalese match is the one TASK.md item I can't close, because it needs a person to listen. I won't claim the condition is met.\n\n**Files**\n- Bundle: `DragonCrateBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-221326.html` in this folder's root.\n- Exit interview: `exit-interview.json`, parses, every field filled.\n- Voice sample for listening: `animalese-sample.wav` in this folder's root. It's a rendered speech of \"Hello there, Builder! Pim waves at you.\" in the granny voice.\n\n**Verified on the final bundle**\n- 119 headless checks pass, including a whole-chapter playthrough with god mode off, player blueprints, and two copies joined over a fake hub bus.\n- A browser run shows attract mode starting after 10 seconds idle, Dev Tools sliders changing and resetting, the Xbox and PlayStation glyphs, and the solo Bee scoring during play.\n- The shared-world round passes the AI Hub harness with 1, 2 and 4 players, a server host, and a host leaving.\n\n**Not verified**\n- Whether the voice sounds like Animalese or Simlish. The voice is formant synthesis, so it is gibberish-shaped rather than speech. If the sample doesn't match what you expect, tell me what's wrong and I'll change the synthesis.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 22:13:26\n- Source: Dragon Quest Builders\n- Plan: PLAN.md (systems and structure). Title: my own 50 riffs, drawn with `secrets.randbelow` (index 18: Dragon Crate Builders)\n- Genre: sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting, town building, a siege and a boss\n- Perspective: third-person isometric; camera turns in quarter steps; three-quarter view on B\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff) with one logged hook; plain JavaScript; Canvas 2D and Web Audio; AI Hub client session.js 1.4.0 unmodified; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art direction: patchwork quilt, stitched fabric block faces, PS1 resolution, procedural furniture and banners\n- Animation: engine rigs, sword moves and blob squash, plus a stitch needle, lantern flicker, banner flutter, and the Builder's cast and roll\n- Audio: music and effects in code; voices formant-synthesised in code, intended as Animalese-style gibberish; the match is not verified by ear\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world mode, one town for 1–4 players\n- Testing: 119 headless checks; a browser run of the final HTML; offline spectral measurements of the voice; the AI Hub harness in shared-world mode\n- Research: none beyond the engine API card and the AI Hub protocol\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium headless via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues found: about 21 in the game, plus several mistakes in my own tests. Late rounds found the voice gap, the missing player-authored patterns, and a browser-driver trap where instant key presses don't register as held.\n- Critical: 2, both fixed. Blocks drawn over the Builder (a depth anchor in the wrong units), and a hub session not opening into the round.\n- Most helpful: the browser run on the final HTML, which showed what a player would see.\n- Least helpful: the engine checker's colour-count notes, which suit bright scenes rather than a dim block world at night.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the rigs with E.MOVES and E.Attack, the standard pause, settings, controls, dialog and menus, and the audio context, which made custom synthesis possible.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: `r.queue` takes world units, and I first passed cell numbers. A scene fade silently holds the scene update. Dialog plays one fixed sound per letter, so per-letter voices needed a hook.\n- Built myself: the voxel world, block rays, room detection, per-block drawing, the shared-round sync, ballistas, portals, patterns and blueprints, and the formant voice.\n- Hardest: stacking blocks in the isometric depth-sorted draw, and keeping a host-run world in step across copies.\n- Engine changes: one. Dialog gained an `onLetter(index, talk)` hook, marked `// DQ:`.\n- One change I'd make: a per-letter voice option in the dialog, so games don't need the hook.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the exact deliverable names, the no-subagent rule, the test-output rule and the hub link.\n- Confusing: the plan, TASK.md and the goal wording pointed different ways, and \"every feature\" only became a checklist through repeated hook feedback.\n- Hardest: fitting the plan into one session. Co-op on a single-player world took more work than the rest of the round combined.\n- Would change: list the required features once, with the evidence expected for each, and mark which judgements need a human listener or tester."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2156,"cache_read_input_tokens":585166,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2156},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82f305e6-f719-4e49-881f-e498d001a34b","uuid":"5fc3cbc6-2b8c-46a8-a261-950f7d5ca952","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:54:02.270Z","request_id":"req_011CghNNeku8ndmNGAEacwzS"}
